\begin_layout Title
Adapting Freedman's Method To A Kolmorov Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By defining the strict Kolmogorov expectation framework of Freedman's Equation (I.3) as an automated Ada function you can see exactly how the 1972 data stacks up against classic local realistic boundaries.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The Mathematics of the Kolmogorov Test 
\begin_inset Formula $(\ensuremath{H_{0}}$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Under your measure-theoretic model,
 the true joint expectation value for two ideal binary analyzers separated by a relative physical angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

 is evaluated using a uniform,
 un-skewed classical probability density.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For an ideal local realistic system,
 the expected normalized coincidence rate tracks a clean linear or harmonic boundary:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E_{i}(\phi) & = & \frac{1}{2}\left[\epsilon^{1}\epsilon^{1}-(\epsilon^{1}-\epsilon^{2})^{2}\cos^{2}(\phi)\right]
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Chi-Square test evaluates how closely Freedman's 9 observed laboratory ratios 
\begin_inset Formula $(\ensuremath{O_{i}})$
\end_inset

 fit this exact strict Kolmogorov expectation across all angles simultaneously:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\chi^{2} & = & \sum^{9}_{i=1}\frac{(O_{i}-E_{i})^{2}}{\sigma^{2}_{i}}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the precise empirical standard deviation Freedman recorded for each row.
\end_layout

